WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:10.950 --> 00:00:14.020
My name is Roland Schwartz and I am a 
computational biologist

00:00:14.020 --> 00:00:15.210
at the European Bioinformatics Institute

00:00:15.800 --> 00:00:18.900
The major part of my research

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.210
is the development of new statistical
approaches and computational methods

00:00:23.210 --> 00:00:27.199
for improving what is called phylogenetic
inference

00:00:27.199 --> 00:00:32.259
the reconstruction of the tree of life
or parts of the tree of life.

00:00:32.259 --> 00:00:35.760
One of the most fascinating things about
DNA for me is that it's digital

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:38.700
and consists of these four characters As, Cs, Ts and Gs

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:41.610
so it lends itself to the analysis with computational methods

00:00:41.610 --> 00:00:44.640
and we're developing and improving those
methods that look

00:00:44.640 --> 00:00:48.170
in DNA sequences for these changes so we
can say

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:51.379
in the course of evolution from animal
1 to animal 2

00:00:51.379 --> 00:00:54.780
one of these As for example in a
certain position in the sequence

00:00:54.780 --> 00:00:55.910
has evolved to a C.

00:00:55.910 --> 00:00:59.410
I don't think there's really a typical
day in my work

00:00:59.410 --> 00:01:03.000
I think it really depends on the project you're working on

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:06.560
so even though I'm a computational
biologist not all of my time is spent

00:01:06.560 --> 00:01:07.590
in front of the computer

00:01:07.590 --> 00:01:11.410
I think an important part of all the
projects that I do are

00:01:11.410 --> 00:01:15.580
meeting the collaborators talking to
people, to biologists and to the medics

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:18.340
to try to understand their point of view
and their problems,

00:01:18.340 --> 00:01:23.820
but also withdrawing into a quiet corner to
read some papers and try to

00:01:23.830 --> 00:01:26.910
get your head around a certain project and problem before you return to the

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:29.680
computer and try to analyse the data
and so on.

00:01:30.640 --> 00:01:33.180
One of the greatest things about my line
of work I think is

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:34.760
how inter-disciplinary it is.

00:01:34.760 --> 00:01:40.120
So apart from my own line research I work with biologists, with clinical scientists

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:42.400
and every time I help them
analyse their data

00:01:42.410 --> 00:01:46.660
help them solve their problems I myself
learn a lot of new biology

00:01:46.660 --> 00:01:49.780
as well as a lot of new computer science
and maths.

00:01:50.100 --> 00:01:56.140
I believe that careers are this sort of evolutionary
processes in the same sense that

00:01:56.150 --> 00:01:59.870
random changes in the genome occur and
give rise to all these new organisms

00:01:59.870 --> 00:02:02.620
I think it has been random changes in my life

00:02:02.620 --> 00:02:05.340
that have made me first go into bioinformatics

00:02:05.350 --> 00:02:08.429
and then later end up here at the EBI in
Cambridge

00:02:08.429 --> 00:02:12.620
and so while I believe that you should
have a rough idea of where you going

00:02:12.620 --> 00:02:15.800
I think you cannot prepare for the
completely unknown.

00:02:16.200 --> 00:02:18.480
If I remember back in my days in school

00:02:18.480 --> 00:02:23.180
while I enjoyed certain subjects of course
I always thought that learning was usually

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:27.070
learned for your parents or for your teachers
or to have good grades but then later

00:02:27.070 --> 00:02:31.130
when I started doing my PhD where I exclusively
could focus on the actual

00:02:31.130 --> 00:02:34.590
research that I was interested in,
suddenly I found it really exciting to

00:02:34.590 --> 00:02:35.660
learn new things and

00:02:35.660 --> 00:02:38.860
I think that's one of the most rewarding
things you can have in a job is too

00:02:38.860 --> 00:02:41.000
get paid for learning.

